107: ## Exposure and the API-key caveat
108: 
109: `bind_address` defaults to `127.0.0.1` (localhost only), and `expose_public`
110: (default `false`) is what would open the ports in iptables.
111: 
112: **`model_api_key` is empty by default, and llama-server's own CORS policy
113: allows every origin.** That is only acceptable because the published port binds
114: to loopback. Before setting `bind_address = "0.0.0.0"`:
115: 
116: ```sh
117: tofu apply -var 'model_api_key=<bearer-token>'
118: ```
119: 
120: Otherwise anything on the LAN gets unauthenticated inference. The server prints
121: a `no API key is set and CORS allows all origins` warning at startup; that line
122: is expected here, not a misconfiguration.
123: 
124: ## Host quirks handled here
125: 
126: These are the non-obvious things that bite on this host, kept in comments at the
127: relevant code:
128: 
129: - **`HealthStartPeriod` must be a Go duration** (`20m`). systemd's `20min` is
130:   rejected by podman.
131: - **No `HealthTimeoutSec`** on this podman (6.1.3); the units use
132:   `HealthStartPeriod` + `HealthRetries` instead.
133: - **`[Container]` is parsed as a map**, so repeated `Exec=` keys collapse to the
134:   last one. The whole llama-server command line is assembled in `main.tf` and
135:   emitted as a single `Exec=`.
136: - **`network-online.target` never activates** on this host (no NetworkManager /
137:   systemd-networkd). Podman gates every Quadlet unit on
138:   `podman-user-wait-network-online.service`, which polls for up to 90 s per
139:   start. A drop-in overriding it to `/bin/true` is installed when
140:   `skip_network_online_wait = true`. Set it to `false` on a host where the
141:   target does work.
142: - **The container bind address goes in `LLAMA_ARG_HOST`**, not `--host`: the
143:   image already defines that variable, and passing both makes llama-server warn
144:   on every boot that one overwrites the other. Setting it to an empty string is
145:   worse — arg parsing fails with `--host requires at least one address`.
146: - **The Qdrant image ships neither `curl` nor `wget`**, so its health probe is a
147:   bash `/dev/tcp` request against `/readyz` rather than an HTTP client.
148: - **`fetch-gguf.sh` probes for the retry flag instead of assuming one.** The
149:   llama.cpp image has curl 8.5.0, which has `--retry-all-errors` and no longer
150:   has `--retry-error`; the script checks for both.
151: 
152: ## Project layout
153: 
154: - `main.tf` — writes storage.conf, the Quadlet units, the rendered schema, and runs the start steps
155: - `variables.tf` — tunables (model, images, ports, credentials, host quirks)
156: - `outputs.tf` — endpoints, storage paths, post-apply steps
157: - `quadlet/` — Quadlet templates (`*.container.tftpl`, `*.pod.tftpl`)
158: - `scripts/fetch-gguf.sh` — resumable download with size + optional sha256 check
159: - `postgres/initdb/01-aigents-schema.sql` — schema, templated on `embedding_dimensions`
160: 
161: ## Teardown
162: 
163: ```sh
164: systemctl --user stop qwen-coder.service postgres.service qdrant.service
165: systemctl --user stop aigents-pod.service
166: tofu destroy
167: ```
